import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Helly
import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Polyhedral.Duality
import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Polyhedral.Homogeneous

/-!
# The refined theorems of the alternative

The alternative for an infinite system of weak convex inequalities $f_i(x) \le 0$ on the whole
space, `ConvexAnalysis.alternative_infinite_system_univ`, assumes that the $f_i$ have no common
direction of recession. This hypothesis can be weakened. It is enough that finitely many of the
$f_i$ are affine and that every common direction of recession is a direction in which all the other
$f_i$ are constant ([rockafellar1970convex], Theorem 21.4). The corresponding refinement of Helly's
theorem asks that finitely many of the closed convex sets $C_i$ are polyhedral and that every common
direction of recession is a direction in which all the other $C_i$ are linear
([rockafellar1970convex], Theorem 21.5). The proofs also use material of [rockafellar1970convex],
§13, §19 and §20.

The refined proof changes exactly one step of the unrefined one. Let $k$ be the positively
homogeneous convex function generated by $\operatorname{conv} \{f_i^*\}$. Both proofs end with
`ConvexAnalysis.exists_multipliers_of_posHomGen_convFn_convexConj_eq_bot` once $k(0) = -\infty$ is
known. The unrefined proof gets $k(0) = -\infty$ from
$0 \in \operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{dom} k)$. Here the family is split into the affine part and
the rest. Let $k_0$ and $k_1$ be the positively homogeneous convex functions generated by the convex
hulls of the conjugates of the two parts. Then $k_0$ is polyhedral, and the separation of
$-\operatorname{dom} k_0$ from $\operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{dom} k_1)$ gives $k(0) = -\infty$.

Only the case of the whole space is treated, as in [rockafellar1970convex]. A version relative to a
closed convex set $C$ needs no new mathematics: the indicator function $\delta(\cdot \mid C)$ is
added to the family. But this version has the hypothesis that $C$ is linear in every common
direction of recession, or that $C$ is polyhedral and is cut into half-spaces.

## Main results

* `ConvexAnalysis.convexConj_affineFn`, `ConvexAnalysis.epi_convexConj_affineFn`: the conjugate of
  $\langle \cdot, a \rangle - c$ is $\delta(\cdot \mid a) + c$, and its epigraph is one translated
  vertical ray. This makes $k_0$ polyhedral.
* `ConvexAnalysis.polyhedralFn_posHomGen_convFn_convexConj_affineFn`: $k_0$ is polyhedral.
* `ConvexAnalysis.convexConj_posHomGen_convFn_convexConj`: $k_j^*$ is the indicator function of the
  solution set $C_j$ of the $j$-th part.
* `ConvexAnalysis.nonempty_neg_convexDom_inter_relint_convexDom`: the separation step,
  $(-\operatorname{dom} k_0) \cap \operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{dom} k_1) \neq \emptyset$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.apply_zero_eq_bot_of_le_of_le`: the main step of the refinement, $k(0) = -\infty$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.alternative_infinite_system_univ_of_affine_tail`: the refined alternative for
  functions.
* `ConvexAnalysis.exists_forall_le_zero_of_forall_subsystem_of_affine_tail`: the corresponding
  criterion for solvability.
* `ConvexAnalysis.helly_of_polyhedral_tail`: the refined Helly theorem.
* `ConvexAnalysis.Polyhedral.exists_finset_pairing`: a polyhedral set is cut out by finitely many
  inequalities of the pairing. This lets the refined Helly theorem give its half-spaces to the
  refined alternative.
* `ConvexAnalysis.constancySpace_indicatorFn`, `ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_affineFn_nonpos_iff`,
  `ConvexAnalysis.mem_recessionCone_of_forall_pairing_nonpos`: the translation between the language
  of sets and the language of functions.
* `ConvexAnalysis.PosHomogeneous.add_le_add_of_ne_top`: a positively homogeneous convex function is
  subadditive at the points where it is less than $+\infty$.

## Implementation notes

* The function $k = \operatorname{conv} \{k_0, k_1\}$ is never formed. Only
  $k(0) \le k_0(-z) + k_1(z)$ is used, so `ConvexAnalysis.apply_zero_eq_bot_of_le_of_le` takes any
  positively homogeneous convex $k$ below both $k_0$ and $k_1$.
* The two parts are indexed by arbitrary types, and neither needs to be nonempty. Rockafellar adds
  functions that are identically zero to avoid empty parts. This is not necessary here, because
  `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen h` is at most $0$ at the origin for every $h$, and for an empty family
  `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen (ConvexAnalysis.convFn g)` is $\delta(\cdot \mid 0)$, which is
  polyhedral with domain $\{0\}$.
* The hypothesis `B.SeparatingRight` replaces the identification of $\mathbb{R}^n$ with its dual in
  [rockafellar1970convex]. It makes the conjugate of an affine function the indicator of a point,
  and not the indicator of an affine subspace.
-/
open Set Pointwise

namespace ConvexAnalysis

section AffineConj

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ}

/-- The conjugate of the affine function $x \mapsto \langle x, a \rangle - c$ takes the value $c$ at
$a$. -/
theorem convexConj_affineFn_apply_self (a : F) (c : ℝ) :
    convexConj B (affineFn B a c) a = ((c : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  rw [convexConj_apply]
  refine le_antisymm (iSup_le fun x => ?_) ?_
  · rw [affineFn_eq_coe, ← EReal.coe_sub]
    exact_mod_cast le_of_eq (by ring)
  · have h := le_iSup (fun x : E => ((B x a : ℝ) : EReal) - affineFn B a c x) 0
    simpa using h

/-- Let the pairing separate the points of $F$. Then the conjugate of
$x \mapsto \langle x, a \rangle - c$ is $+\infty$ at every $y \neq a$, because the pairing
separates $y - a$ from $0$, so $\langle \cdot, y - a \rangle$ is unbounded above. -/
theorem convexConj_affineFn_apply_of_ne (hB : B.SeparatingRight) {a y : F} (hy : y ≠ a) (c : ℝ) :
    convexConj B (affineFn B a c) y = ⊤ := by
  obtain ⟨x₀, hx₀⟩ : ∃ x : E, B x (y - a) ≠ 0 := by
    by_contra hcon
    push Not at hcon
    exact hy (sub_eq_zero.1 (hB _ hcon))
  set t : ℝ := B x₀ (y - a) with ht
  rw [convexConj_apply, EReal.eq_top_iff_forall_lt]
  intro d
  have hval : ∀ s : ℝ, ((B (s • x₀) y : ℝ) : EReal) - affineFn B a c (s • x₀)
      = ((s * t + c : ℝ) : EReal) := by
    intro s
    rw [affineFn_eq_coe, ← EReal.coe_sub]
    refine congrArg _ ?_
    have h1 : B (s • x₀) y = s * B x₀ y := by rw [map_smul]; simp
    have h2 : B (s • x₀) a = s * B x₀ a := by rw [map_smul]; simp
    have h3 : t = B x₀ y - B x₀ a := by rw [ht, map_sub]
    rw [h1, h2, h3]; ring
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ : ∃ s : ℝ, d < s * t + c := by
    rcases lt_or_gt_of_ne hx₀ with hneg | hpos
    · exact ⟨(d + 1 - c) / t, by rw [div_mul_cancel₀ _ (ne_of_lt hneg)]; linarith⟩
    · exact ⟨(d + 1 - c) / t, by rw [div_mul_cancel₀ _ (ne_of_gt hpos)]; linarith⟩
  refine lt_of_lt_of_le ?_ (le_iSup (fun x : E => ((B x y : ℝ) : EReal) - affineFn B a c x)
    (s • x₀))
  rw [hval s]
  exact_mod_cast hs

/-- **The conjugate of an affine function is a translated indicator of a point.** Let
$B \colon E \times F \to \mathbb{R}$ be a pairing that separates the points of $F$, let
$a \in F$ and $c \in \mathbb{R}$, and let $f(x) = \langle x, a \rangle - c$. Then
$$f^*(y) = \delta(y \mid a) + c \quad \text{for all } y \in F.$$

The hypothesis that the pairing separates the points of $F$ replaces the identification of
$\mathbb{R}^n$ with its dual in [rockafellar1970convex]. The function $f$ is
`ConvexAnalysis.affineFn B a c`. -/
theorem convexConj_affineFn (hB : B.SeparatingRight) (a : F) (c : ℝ) :
    convexConj B (affineFn B a c) = indicatorFn ({a} : Set F) + fun _ => ((c : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  funext y
  by_cases hy : y = a
  · subst hy
    rw [convexConj_affineFn_apply_self]
    simp
  · rw [convexConj_affineFn_apply_of_ne hB hy]
    simp [indicatorFn_of_notMem (by simpa using hy : y ∉ ({a} : Set F))]

/-- **The epigraph of the conjugate of an affine function is one translated vertical ray.** Let the
pairing separate the points of $F$, and let $f(x) = \langle x, a \rangle - c$. Then
$$\operatorname{epi} f^* = (a, c) + \{0\} \times [0, +\infty).$$

This is the form of hypothesis of `ConvexAnalysis.epi_convFn_of_epi_eq`, and it makes the
function $k_0$ of Rockafellar finitely generated. The ray is `ConvexAnalysis.verticalRay F`. -/
theorem epi_convexConj_affineFn (hB : B.SeparatingRight) (a : F) (c : ℝ) :
    epi (convexConj B (affineFn B a c)) = {((a, c) : F × ℝ)} + (verticalRay F : Set (F × ℝ)) := by
  ext p
  rw [mem_epi]
  constructor
  · intro hp
    by_cases hy : p.1 = a
    · rw [hy, convexConj_affineFn_apply_self] at hp
      have hcp : (c : ℝ) ≤ p.2 := by exact_mod_cast hp
      refine ⟨(a, c), rfl, ((0 : F), p.2 - c),
        mem_verticalRay_iff.2 ⟨p.2 - c, by linarith, rfl⟩, ?_⟩
      refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_
      · simpa using hy.symm
      · simp
    · rw [convexConj_affineFn_apply_of_ne hB hy] at hp
      exact absurd hp (by simp)
  · rintro ⟨u, hu, v, hv, rfl⟩
    rw [Set.mem_singleton_iff] at hu
    obtain ⟨d, hd, rfl⟩ := mem_verticalRay_iff.1 hv
    subst hu
    have hfst : (((a, c) : F × ℝ) + ((0 : F), d)).1 = a := by simp
    have hsnd : (((a, c) : F × ℝ) + ((0 : F), d)).2 = c + d := by simp
    rw [hfst, hsnd, convexConj_affineFn_apply_self]
    exact_mod_cast le_add_of_nonneg_right hd

end AffineConj

section PosHomGenAux

variable {E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] {f g : E → EReal}

/-- **A positively homogeneous convex function is subadditive where it is not $+\infty$.** Let
$g \colon E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ be positively homogeneous and convex, and let
$g(x) < +\infty$ and $g(y) < +\infty$. Then
$$g(x + y) \le g(x) + g(y).$$

The usual form of this result asks instead that $g$ never takes the value $-\infty$. This
hypothesis is not available here, because the functions $k_j$ built below can be improper. The
epigraph, a convex cone, gives subadditivity directly wherever both values are less than
$+\infty$. The hypothesis cannot be dropped: on $\mathbb{R}^2$, the function with epigraph
$\{(s, t, \mu) \mid s > 0\} \cup \{(0, 0, \mu) \mid \mu \ge 0\}$ is positively homogeneous and
convex and vanishes at the origin, but $g(0, 0) = 0 > -\infty = g(-1, 0) + g(1, 0)$. -/
theorem PosHomogeneous.add_le_add_of_ne_top (hg : PosHomogeneous g) (hgc : ConvexFn g) {x y : E}
    (hx : g x ≠ ⊤) (hy : g y ≠ ⊤) : g (x + y) ≤ g x + g y := by
  have key : ∀ μ ν : ℝ, g x ≤ ((μ : ℝ) : EReal) → g y ≤ ((ν : ℝ) : EReal) →
      g (x + y) ≤ ((μ + ν : ℝ) : EReal) := by
    intro μ ν hμ hν
    have h : ((x, μ) + (y, ν) : E × ℝ) ∈ epi g :=
      (hg.epiCone hgc).add_mem (mk_mem_epi.2 hμ) (mk_mem_epi.2 hν)
    rw [Prod.mk_add_mk] at h
    exact mk_mem_epi.1 h
  have hxr : g x ≤ (((g x).toReal : ℝ) : EReal) := EReal.le_coe_toReal hx
  have hyr : g y ≤ (((g y).toReal : ℝ) : EReal) := EReal.le_coe_toReal hy
  rcases eq_or_ne (g x) ⊥ with hxb | hxb
  · rw [hxb, EReal.bot_add, le_bot_iff, EReal.eq_bot_iff_forall_lt]
    intro r
    refine lt_of_le_of_lt (key (r - (g y).toReal - 1) (g y).toReal (by rw [hxb]; simp) hyr) ?_
    have hr : (r - (g y).toReal - 1 + (g y).toReal : ℝ) < r := by linarith
    exact_mod_cast hr
  rcases eq_or_ne (g y) ⊥ with hyb | hyb
  · rw [hyb, EReal.add_bot, le_bot_iff, EReal.eq_bot_iff_forall_lt]
    intro r
    refine lt_of_le_of_lt (key (g x).toReal (r - (g x).toReal - 1) hxr (by rw [hyb]; simp)) ?_
    have hr : ((g x).toReal + (r - (g x).toReal - 1) : ℝ) < r := by linarith
    exact_mod_cast hr
  · have hxe : g x = (((g x).toReal : ℝ) : EReal) := (EReal.coe_toReal hx hxb).symm
    have hye : g y = (((g y).toReal : ℝ) : EReal) := (EReal.coe_toReal hy hyb).symm
    rw [hxe, hye, ← EReal.coe_add]
    exact key _ _ hxr hyr

end PosHomGenAux

section Domains

variable {E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] {ι : Sort*}

/-- **The effective domain of the convex hull of a family** is the convex hull of the union of the
effective domains:
$$\operatorname{dom}(\operatorname{conv} \{g_i\}) = \operatorname{conv} \Bigl(\bigcup_i
  \operatorname{dom} g_i\Bigr).$$
The projection onto the first factor is linear, so it maps the convex hull of the union of the
epigraphs to the convex hull of the union of their projections. -/
theorem convexDom_convFn (g : ι → E → EReal) :
    convexDom (convFn g) = convexHull ℝ (⋃ i, convexDom (g i)) := by
  have hfst : (Prod.fst : E × ℝ → E) = ⇑(LinearMap.fst ℝ E ℝ) := rfl
  rw [convFn, convexDom_ofEpi, hfst, LinearMap.image_convexHull, Set.image_iUnion]
  exact congrArg _ (Set.iUnion_congr fun i => (convexDom_eq_fst_image_epi (g i)).symm)

/-- **A linear inequality that holds on every $\operatorname{dom} g_i$ holds on the domain of the
positively homogeneous function generated by $\operatorname{conv} \{g_i\}$.** If $\psi$ is a
linear functional with $\psi \le 0$ on $\operatorname{dom} g_i$ for every $i$, then $\psi \le 0$
on $\operatorname{dom}(\operatorname{posHomGen}(\operatorname{conv} \{g_i\}))$.

The domain of $k_1$ is the convex cone generated by the sets $\operatorname{dom} f_i^*$. This is
the only consequence of that description that the refinement uses. -/
theorem convexDom_posHomGen_convFn_subset (ψ : E →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) {g : ι → E → EReal}
    (hg : ∀ i, convexDom (g i) ⊆ {y : E | ψ y ≤ 0}) :
    convexDom (posHomGen (convFn g)) ⊆ {y : E | ψ y ≤ 0} := by
  have hconv : Convex ℝ {y : E | ψ y ≤ 0} := convex_halfSpace_le ψ.isLinear 0
  have hhull : convexDom (convFn g) ⊆ {y : E | ψ y ≤ 0} := by
    rw [convexDom_convFn]
    exact convexHull_min (Set.iUnion_subset hg) hconv
  rw [convexDom_posHomGen (convexFn_convFn g)]
  rintro y (hy | hy)
  · rw [Set.mem_singleton_iff] at hy
    simp [hy]
  · obtain ⟨a, ha, u, hu, rfl⟩ := by simpa using hy
    change ψ (a • u) ≤ 0
    rw [map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
    exact mul_nonpos_of_nonneg_of_nonpos ha.le (hhull hu)

end Domains

section Halves

variable {E F : Type*}
  [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
  [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace ℝ F] [FiniteDimensional ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ}

omit [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ F] in
/-- **Directions of recession from the conjugate.** Let $B \colon E \times F \to \mathbb{R}$ be a
compatible pairing, and let $g$ be closed proper convex on $E$. Then $y \in E$ is a direction of
recession of $g$, that is $(g 0^+)(y) \le 0$, if and only if $\langle y, x' \rangle \le 0$ for
every $x' \in \operatorname{dom} g^*$. -/
theorem recessionFn_nonpos_iff_forall_mem_convexDom_convexConj [IsCompatiblePairing B]
    {g : E → EReal}
    (hg : ClosedProperConvexFn g) (y : E) :
    recessionFn g y ≤ 0 ↔ ∀ x' ∈ convexDom (convexConj B g), B y x' ≤ 0 := by
  rw [recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hg]
  constructor
  · intro h x' hx'
    have h1 := le_trans (le_supportFn (B := B.flip) hx' y) h
    exact_mod_cast h1
  · intro h
    have h1 : supportFn B.flip (convexDom (convexConj B g)) y ≤ ((0 : ℝ) : EReal) :=
      supportFn_le_coe_iff.2 fun x' hx' => h x' hx'
    simpa using h1

omit [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ F] in
/-- **$k_j^*$ is the indicator function of $C_j$.** Let $B \colon E \times F \to \mathbb{R}$ be a
compatible pairing, and let $g_i$, $i \in I$, be closed proper convex functions on $E$. Let $k$
be the positively homogeneous convex function generated by
$\operatorname{conv} \{g_i^* \mid i \in I\}$. Then the conjugate of $k$, with respect to the
transposed pairing, is
$$k^* = \delta\bigl(\cdot \mid \{x \in E \mid g_i(x) \le 0 \text{ for every } i\}\bigr).$$

The conjugate of a convex hull is the pointwise supremum of the conjugates, and the
**Fenchel–Moreau theorem** closes the argument. The result is used for both $k_0$ and $k_1$. -/
theorem convexConj_posHomGen_convFn_convexConj [IsCompatiblePairing B]
    {ι : Type*} {g : ι → E → EReal} (hg : ∀ i, ClosedProperConvexFn (g i)) :
    convexConj B.flip (posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g i)))
      = indicatorFn {x : E | ∀ i, g i x ≤ 0} := by
  have hconj : convexConj B.flip (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g i)) = ⨆ i, g i := by
    rw [convexConj_convFn]
    exact iSup_congr fun i => convexBiconj_eq_self (hg i).convex (hg i).closed
  rw [convexConj_posHomGen, hconj]
  congr 1
  ext x
  simp only [Set.mem_ofPred_eq, iSup_apply]
  exact ⟨fun hx i => le_trans (le_iSup (fun j => g j x) i) hx, fun hx => iSup_le hx⟩

omit [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] in
/-- **The affine part $k_0$ is polyhedral.** Let $E$ be a real normed space, let $F$ be a
finite-dimensional real normed space, and let $B \colon E \times F \to \mathbb{R}$ be a pairing
that separates the points of $F$. Let $g_i$, $i \in I$, be finitely many affine functions of the
form $g_i(x) = \langle x, a_i \rangle - c_i$. Then the positively homogeneous convex function
generated by $\operatorname{conv} \{g_i^*\}$ is polyhedral.

A convex hull of finitely many polyhedral epigraphs is polyhedral, and by
`ConvexAnalysis.epi_convexConj_affineFn` each $g_i^*$ is the indicator of a point, whose
epigraph is one translated vertical ray. -/
theorem polyhedralFn_posHomGen_convFn_convexConj_affineFn (hB : B.SeparatingRight)
    {ι : Type*} [Finite ι] {g : ι → E → EReal}
    (hg : ∀ i, ∃ (a : F) (c : ℝ), g i = affineFn B a c) :
    PolyhedralFn (posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g i))) := by
  choose a c hac using hg
  refine polyhedralFn_posHomGen_convFn (p := fun i => (a i, c i)) fun i => ?_
  rw [hac i]
  exact epi_convexConj_affineFn hB (a i) (c i)

omit [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] in
/-- **The separation step.** Let $E$ be a real normed space, let $F$ be a finite-dimensional real
normed space, and let $B \colon E \times F \to \mathbb{R}$ be a pairing that is compatible in
both directions. Let $g_{0,i}$, $i \in I_0$, and $g_{1,i}$, $i \in I_1$, be two families of
closed proper convex functions on $E$, and let $k_j$ be the positively homogeneous convex
function generated by $\operatorname{conv} \{g_{j,i}^* \mid i \in I_j\}$. Suppose that $k_0$ is
polyhedral, and that every common direction of recession $y$ of all the $g_{0,i}$ and $g_{1,i}$
is also a direction of recession of every $g_{1,i}$ in the opposite direction $-y$. Then
$$(-\operatorname{dom} k_0) \cap \operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{dom} k_1) \neq \emptyset.$$

Both sets contain the origin, so a separating hyperplane passes through it. Because
$\operatorname{dom} k_0$ is polyhedral, the sets can fail to meet only if they can be separated
properly by a hyperplane that does not contain $\operatorname{dom} k_1$. The normal of this
hyperplane would be a common direction of recession of the whole family, so a direction in which
the $g_{1,i}$ are constant, and then the hyperplane would contain $\operatorname{dom} k_1$. -/
theorem nonempty_neg_convexDom_inter_relint_convexDom [IsCompatiblePairing B]
    [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip]
    {ι₀ ι₁ : Type*} {g₀ : ι₀ → E → EReal} {g₁ : ι₁ → E → EReal}
    (hg₀ : ∀ i, ClosedProperConvexFn (g₀ i)) (hg₁ : ∀ i, ClosedProperConvexFn (g₁ i))
    (hpoly : PolyhedralFn (posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₀ i))))
    (hrec : ∀ y : E, (∀ i, recessionFn (g₀ i) y ≤ 0) → (∀ i, recessionFn (g₁ i) y ≤ 0) →
      ∀ i, recessionFn (g₁ i) (-y) ≤ 0) :
    ((-convexDom (posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₀ i)))) ∩
      ri (convexDom (posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₁ i))))).Nonempty := by
  classical
  set k₀ : F → EReal := posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₀ i)) with hk₀
  set k₁ : F → EReal := posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₁ i)) with hk₁
  have hmem₀ : (0 : F) ∈ convexDom k₀ :=
    mem_convexDom.2 (lt_of_le_of_ne le_top (posHomGen_apply_zero_ne_top _))
  have hmem₁ : (0 : F) ∈ convexDom k₁ :=
    mem_convexDom.2 (lt_of_le_of_ne le_top (posHomGen_apply_zero_ne_top _))
  have hsub₀ : ∀ i, convexDom (convexConj B (g₀ i)) ⊆ convexDom k₀ := fun i y hy =>
    lt_of_le_of_lt (le_trans (posHomGen_le _ y) (convFn_le _ i y)) hy
  have hsub₁ : ∀ i, convexDom (convexConj B (g₁ i)) ⊆ convexDom k₁ := fun i y hy =>
    lt_of_le_of_lt (le_trans (posHomGen_le _ y) (convFn_le _ i y)) hy
  by_contra hcon
  rw [Set.not_nonempty_iff_eq_empty] at hcon
  obtain ⟨φ, c, hsep, hns⟩ := (exists_separates_not_subset_iff_disjoint_relint
    hpoly.polyhedral_convexDom.neg (convexFn_posHomGen _).convex_convexDom ⟨0, hmem₁⟩).2
    (Set.disjoint_iff_inter_eq_empty.2 hcon)
  have hmemneg : (0 : F) ∈ -convexDom k₀ := by simpa using hmem₀
  have hc0 : c = 0 := by
    have h1 : φ 0 ≤ c := hsep.le_of_mem_left hmemneg
    have h2 : c ≤ φ 0 := hsep.le_of_mem_right hmem₁
    rw [map_zero] at h1 h2
    linarith
  obtain ⟨w, hw, hwne⟩ : ∃ w ∈ convexDom k₁, φ w ≠ c := by
    by_contra hcon2
    push Not at hcon2
    exact hns fun w hw => hcon2 w hw
  have hwpos : 0 < φ w := lt_of_le_of_ne (hc0 ▸ hsep.le_of_mem_right hw) (Ne.symm (hc0 ▸ hwne))
  obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := exists_pairing_eq B.flip φ
  have hrec₀ : ∀ i, recessionFn (g₀ i) (-y) ≤ 0 := by
    intro i
    rw [recessionFn_nonpos_iff_forall_mem_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) (hg₀ i)]
    intro x' hx'
    have hmem : -x' ∈ -convexDom k₀ := by simpa using hsub₀ i hx'
    have h1 : φ (-x') ≤ c := hsep.le_of_mem_left hmem
    rw [hy (-x'), hc0] at h1
    have h2 : B y (-x') ≤ 0 := h1
    rw [map_neg] at h2 ⊢
    simpa using h2
  have hrec₁ : ∀ i, recessionFn (g₁ i) (-y) ≤ 0 := by
    intro i
    rw [recessionFn_nonpos_iff_forall_mem_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) (hg₁ i)]
    intro x' hx'
    have h1 : c ≤ φ x' := hsep.le_of_mem_right (hsub₁ i hx')
    rw [hy x', hc0] at h1
    have h2 : (0 : ℝ) ≤ B y x' := h1
    rw [map_neg]
    simpa using h2
  have hback : ∀ i, recessionFn (g₁ i) y ≤ 0 := by
    have h := hrec (-y) hrec₀ hrec₁
    simpa using h
  have hdom₁ : convexDom k₁ ⊆ {x' : F | (B y) x' ≤ 0} :=
    convexDom_posHomGen_convFn_subset (B y) fun i x' hx' =>
      (recessionFn_nonpos_iff_forall_mem_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) (hg₁ i) y).1 (hback i) x' hx'
  have hfin : B y w ≤ 0 := hdom₁ hw
  rw [hy w] at hwpos
  exact absurd hfin (not_le.2 hwpos)

omit [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] in
/-- If $g$ is a polyhedral convex function, then $y \mapsto g(-y)$ is polyhedral. -/
theorem PolyhedralFn.comp_neg {g : F → EReal} (hg : PolyhedralFn g) :
    PolyhedralFn (fun y => g (-y)) := by
  have h : Polyhedral (epi (fun y => g (-y))) := by
    rw [epi_comp_neg]
    exact Polyhedral.image hg _
  exact h

/-- **The main step of the refinement.** Let $E$ and $F$ be finite-dimensional real normed spaces,
and let $B \colon E \times F \to \mathbb{R}$ be a pairing that is compatible in both directions.
Let $g_{0,i}$, $i \in I_0$, and $g_{1,i}$, $i \in I_1$, be two families of closed proper convex
functions on $E$, and let $k_j$ be the positively homogeneous convex function generated by
$\operatorname{conv} \{g_{j,i}^* \mid i \in I_j\}$. Suppose that

* $k_0$ is polyhedral;
* every common direction of recession $y$ of all the $g_{0,i}$ and $g_{1,i}$ is also a direction
  of recession of every $g_{1,i}$ in the direction $-y$;
* there is no $x \in E$ with $g_{0,i}(x) \le 0$ and $g_{1,i}(x) \le 0$ for all $i$.

Let $k \colon F \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ be positively homogeneous and convex with $k \le k_0$
and $k \le k_1$. Then $k(0) = -\infty$.

Separation gives a point $z \in (-\operatorname{dom} k_0) \cap
\operatorname{ri}(\operatorname{dom} k_1)$. If $k_0$ or $k_1$ is improper there, then
$k(0) = -\infty$ at once. Otherwise the conjugate of the sum $k_0(-\cdot) + k_1$ is the infimal
convolution of the two conjugates, which are the indicators of the two solution sets, and these
sets have empty intersection. Rockafellar applies this to
$k = \operatorname{conv} \{k_0, k_1\}$, but only through $k(0) \le k_0(-z) + k_1(z)$, which
needs nothing of $k$ beyond $k \le k_0$, $k \le k_1$ and subadditivity. -/
theorem apply_zero_eq_bot_of_le_of_le [IsCompatiblePairing B] [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip]
    {ι₀ ι₁ : Type*} {g₀ : ι₀ → E → EReal} {g₁ : ι₁ → E → EReal}
    (hg₀ : ∀ i, ClosedProperConvexFn (g₀ i)) (hg₁ : ∀ i, ClosedProperConvexFn (g₁ i))
    (hpoly : PolyhedralFn (posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₀ i))))
    (hrec : ∀ y : E, (∀ i, recessionFn (g₀ i) y ≤ 0) → (∀ i, recessionFn (g₁ i) y ≤ 0) →
      ∀ i, recessionFn (g₁ i) (-y) ≤ 0)
    (hempty : ¬ ∃ x : E, (∀ i, g₀ i x ≤ 0) ∧ (∀ i, g₁ i x ≤ 0))
    {k : F → EReal} (hk : PosHomogeneous k) (hkc : ConvexFn k)
    (hle₀ : k ≤ posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₀ i)))
    (hle₁ : k ≤ posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₁ i))) :
    k 0 = ⊥ := by
  classical
  set k₀ : F → EReal := posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₀ i)) with hk₀def
  set k₁ : F → EReal := posHomGen (convFn fun i => convexConj B (g₁ i)) with hk₁def
  obtain ⟨z, hz₀, hz₁⟩ := nonempty_neg_convexDom_inter_relint_convexDom hg₀ hg₁ hpoly hrec
  have hzd₀ : k₀ (-z) < ⊤ := mem_convexDom.1 (Set.mem_neg.1 hz₀)
  have hzd₁ : k₁ z < ⊤ := mem_convexDom.1 (intrinsicInterior_subset hz₁)
  -- Subadditivity of `k` at the points where both `kⱼ` are finite.
  have hsub : ∀ w : F, k₀ (-w) ≠ ⊤ → k₁ w ≠ ⊤ → k 0 ≤ k₀ (-w) + k₁ w := by
    intro w hw₀ hw₁
    have h1 : k (-w) ≠ ⊤ := fun hc => hw₀ (top_le_iff.1 (hc ▸ hle₀ (-w)))
    have h2 : k w ≠ ⊤ := fun hc => hw₁ (top_le_iff.1 (hc ▸ hle₁ w))
    have h3 := hk.add_le_add_of_ne_top hkc h1 h2
    rw [neg_add_cancel] at h3
    exact le_trans h3 (add_le_add (hle₀ (-w)) (hle₁ w))
  by_cases hb : k₀ (-z) = ⊥ ∨ k₁ z = ⊥
  · refine le_bot_iff.1 ?_
    have h := hsub z (ne_of_lt hzd₀) (ne_of_lt hzd₁)
    rcases hb with hb | hb
    · rwa [hb, EReal.bot_add] at h
    · rwa [hb, EReal.add_bot] at h
  push Not at hb
  -- Both `kⱼ` are proper: `k₀` is polyhedral hence lower semicontinuous, and `z` is a relative
  -- interior point of `dom k₁`.
  have hp₀ : ProperConvex k₀ := by
    by_contra hnp
    exact hb.1 (ConvexFn.eq_bot_of_mem_closure_convexDom (convexFn_posHomGen _)
      hpoly.lowerSemicontinuous hnp (subset_closure (Set.mem_neg.1 hz₀)))
  have hp₁ : ProperConvex k₁ := by
    by_contra hnp
    exact hb.2 (ConvexFn.eq_bot_of_mem_relint_convexDom (convexFn_posHomGen _) hnp hz₁)
  -- the conjugate of a sum splits as an infimal convolution, for `g = k₀(-·)` and `k₁`.
  have hgpoly : PolyhedralFn (fun w : F => k₀ (-w)) := hpoly.comp_neg
  have hgproper : ProperConvex (fun w : F => k₀ (-w)) := hp₀.comp_neg
  have hgdom : z ∈ convexDom (fun w : F => k₀ (-w)) := by rw [convexDom_comp_neg]; exact hz₀
  have hexact : IsExactSum B.flip (fun w : F => k₀ (-w)) k₁ :=
    IsExactSum.of_polyhedral hgpoly hgproper (convexFn_posHomGen _) hp₁ hgdom hz₁
  -- the two conjugates are the indicators of the two solution sets
  have hconj₀ : convexConj B.flip k₀ = indicatorFn {x : E | ∀ i, g₀ i x ≤ 0} :=
    convexConj_posHomGen_convFn_convexConj hg₀
  have hconj₁ : convexConj B.flip k₁ = indicatorFn {x : E | ∀ i, g₁ i x ≤ 0} :=
    convexConj_posHomGen_convFn_convexConj hg₁
  have htop : convexConj B.flip ((fun w : F => k₀ (-w)) + k₁) 0 = ⊤ := by
    rw [hexact.convexConj_add_apply]
    refine le_antisymm le_top (le_iInf fun y => ?_)
    rw [zero_sub, convexConj_comp_neg, neg_neg, hconj₀, hconj₁]
    have hne : ¬ ((∀ i, g₀ i y ≤ 0) ∧ ∀ i, g₁ i y ≤ 0) := fun hc => hempty ⟨y, hc⟩
    rcases not_and_or.1 hne with h | h
    · rw [indicatorFn_of_notMem (show y ∉ {x : E | ∀ i, g₀ i x ≤ 0} from h)]
      exact le_of_eq (EReal.top_add_of_ne_bot (indicatorFn_ne_bot _ y)).symm
    · rw [indicatorFn_of_notMem (show y ∉ {x : E | ∀ i, g₁ i x ≤ 0} from h)]
      exact le_of_eq (EReal.add_top_of_ne_bot (indicatorFn_ne_bot _ y)).symm
  -- `k(0)` is below the sum everywhere, and the sum has infimum `-∞`.
  have hfinal : ∀ w : F, k 0 ≤ ((fun w : F => k₀ (-w)) + k₁) w := by
    intro w
    rw [Pi.add_apply]
    by_cases hw₀ : k₀ (-w) = ⊤
    · rw [hw₀, EReal.top_add_of_ne_bot (hp₁.ne_bot w)]
      exact le_top
    by_cases hw₁ : k₁ w = ⊤
    · rw [hw₁, EReal.add_top_of_ne_bot (hp₀.ne_bot (-w))]
      exact le_top
    exact hsub w hw₀ hw₁
  have hle : convexConj B.flip ((fun w : F => k₀ (-w)) + k₁) 0 ≤ -(k 0) := by
    rw [convexConj_apply]
    refine iSup_le fun w => ?_
    have h0 : ((B.flip w 0 : ℝ) : EReal) = 0 := by simp
    rw [h0, zero_sub]
    exact EReal.neg_le_neg_iff.2 (hfinal w)
  rw [htop] at hle
  have h2 : -(k 0) = ⊤ := top_le_iff.1 hle
  have h3 := congrArg (fun a : EReal => -a) h2
  simpa using h3

